menu "Detector Wi-Fi dashboard"

    config FY_WIFI_DASHBOARD
        bool "Offer the Wi-Fi dashboard"
        default y if FY_BOARD_HELTEC_V43
        default n
        help
            A WPA3 access point serving the dashboard, started from the
            Device menu (or, on boards without a display, by five quick
            button presses). Detection is paused while it runs. The XIAO
            uses its USB dashboard instead.

    config FY_DASHBOARD_SSID_PREFIX
        string "Access point name prefix"
        default "FY-"
        help
            The access point is named this prefix followed by four random
            characters chosen on first use, for example FY-K7QM. Avoid names
            that announce what the device is.

    config FY_DASHBOARD_PASSKEY
        string "Shared access point passkey (empty: one per device)"
        default ""
        help
            Empty: each device generates its own temporary passkey on first
            use and shows it on its display. Boards without a display (the
            XIAO) use this shared passkey instead; it must be 8-63
            characters. Anyone who knows a shared passkey can join any
            device built with it while that device's dashboard is on.

    config FY_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD
        string "Default dashboard password"
        default "changeme"
        help
            Every device starts with this password for the USB and Wi-Fi
            dashboards (8-64 characters). The first login with it leads to
            a page that requires a new password before anything else opens;
            the new one is stored on the device as a salted PBKDF2 hash.

    config FY_DASHBOARD_CHANNEL
        int "Access point channel"
        default 6
        range 1 13

    config FY_DASHBOARD_IDLE_S
        int "End a dashboard session after this many seconds without use"
        default 600
        range 60 3600
        help
            A session ends when nobody has pressed the button or used the
            dashboard for this long. Detection then resumes in the selected
            mode. The page's own background checks do not count, so an
            open browser tab does not keep the session alive.

    config FY_DASHBOARD_CAPTIVE_PORTAL
        bool "Present the dashboards as a captive portal"
        default y
        help
            Phones open the dashboard in their "sign in to network"
            window, without turning off mobile data. The dashboard networks'
            DHCP servers also offer the device as gateway and DNS server, a
            DNS server answers every name with the device's address, and the
            HTTP server redirects everything except the dashboard there,
            including operating systems' connectivity checks, which do not
            count as dashboard activity. A PC on the USB link therefore also
            gets a default route and DNS server there (normally outranked by
            its other connections). Off: the networks offer an address only,
            and Android reaches the page only with mobile data and Wi-Fi off.

    config FY_DASHBOARD_HOST
        string "Dashboard host name"
        default "dash.fy"
        depends on FY_DASHBOARD_CAPTIVE_PORTAL
        help
            A name for the dashboard on a phone connected to the device:
            the portal's DNS answers it (like every name) with the device's
            address, and the server treats it as the device, so
            http://dash.fy/ opens the dashboard. Computers that ask their
            own DNS servers first may not resolve it; the address always
            works.

endmenu
